[[knowledge-graph]]
The code knowledge graph

A knowledge graph turns a source tree into nodes and typed edges. Every function,
type, module, and document section becomes a node identified by a stable anchor, and
every relationship between them — imports, calls, definitions, cross-references —
becomes an edge with a declared type. The anchor is what lets a sentence in a design
document point at the exact symbol it describes.

Representing the codebase this way makes questions answerable by traversal instead of
by reading files. Where a flat tag file can only tell you where a name is defined, a
typed graph can walk from a symbol to its callers, from a contract to the code that
must satisfy it, or from a paragraph of prose to the implementation it documents. The
graph is the substrate every other analysis is built on.
